%S A400596 1,2,3,4,5,16,17,37,64,36,35,61,97,139,191,251,193,141,98,62,34,14,13,
%T A400596 29,11,9,8,7,19,41,71,43,73,109,72,74,113,75,44,21,20,6,18,38,67,103,
%U A400596 149,104,68,39,40,69,107,151,106,152,108,153,205,206,207,269
%N A400596 Squares visited by a chess king moving on the square spiral, always moving to an adjacent unvisited square whose number has the fewest distinct prime factors; ties are resolved by choosing the lowest number.
%C A400596 The board is numbered by the standard square spiral, with 1 at the origin, 2 immediately to its right, and 3 above 2. The king starts at square 1. At each step it moves to one of the eight adjacent squares that has not previously been visited.
%C A400596 The sequence is finite. The last term is a(93) = 22.
%D A400596 a(2) = 2. The squares adjacent to 1 are numbered 2 through 9. The numbers 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8 and 9 each have one distinct prime factor, while 6 has two; among those attaining the minimum, 2 is the smallest.
%D A400596 Starting from a(8) = 37, the unvisited squares adjacent to 37 are 36, 38, 63, 64, 65, 66 and 67. The numbers 36, 38, 63 and 65 each have two distinct prime factors, 66 has three, while 64 and 67 each have only one; among those attaining the minimum, 64 is the smallest. Hence a(9) = 64.
%H A400596 Bartlomiej Pawlik, <a href="/A400596/b400596.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..93</a>
%t A400596 Module[{d=DeleteCases[Tuples[{-1,0,1},2],{0,0}],u,w,p={0,0},s={1},a,c},u[{x_,y_}]:=1+Which[y>=Abs[x],4y^2-y-x,-x>=Abs[y],4x^2-x-y,-y>=Abs[x],(4y-3)y+x,True,(4x-3)x+y];w[1]=0;w[n_]:=Length[FactorInteger[n]];While[True,c=Select[{u[#],#}&/@((p+#)&/@d),!MemberQ[s,#[[1]]]&];If[c=={},Break[]];a=First@SortBy[c,{w[#[[1]]]&,#[[1]]&}];p=a[[2]];AppendTo[s,a[[1]]]];s]
%Y A400596 Cf. A001221, A335816, A333713, A335856, A335661.
%K A400596 nonn,walk,fini,full,new
%O A400596 1,2
